A dictionary alone can find a value quickly, but it does not tell us which entry is oldest. The linked list handles that order. The node after `head` is the newest entry, while the node before `tail` is the one that should be evicted.

The code changes only its own internal nodes. It does not modify any input array or collection.

```csharp
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class LRUCache {
  private class Node {
    public int Key;
    public int Value;
    public Node Prev;
    public Node Next;

    public Node(int key, int value) {
      Key = key;
      Value = value;
    }
  }

  private readonly int capacity;
  private readonly Dictionary<int, Node> cache;
  private readonly Node head;
  private readonly Node tail;

  public LRUCache(int capacity) {
    this.capacity = capacity;
    cache = new Dictionary<int, Node>();
    head = new Node(0, 0);
    tail = new Node(0, 0);
    head.Next = tail;
    tail.Prev = head;
  }

  private void Remove(Node node) {
    node.Prev.Next = node.Next;
    node.Next.Prev = node.Prev;
  }

  private void AddToFront(Node node) {
    node.Next = head.Next;
    node.Prev = head;
    head.Next.Prev = node;
    head.Next = node;
  }

  public int Get(int key) {
    if (!cache.TryGetValue(key, out Node node)) {
      return -1;
    }

    Remove(node);
    AddToFront(node);
    return node.Value;
  }

  public void Put(int key, int value) {
    if (capacity == 0) {
      return;
    }

    if (cache.TryGetValue(key, out Node existing)) {
      existing.Value = value;
      Remove(existing);
      AddToFront(existing);
      return;
    }

    if (cache.Count == capacity) {
      Node leastRecent = tail.Prev;
      Remove(leastRecent);
      cache.Remove(leastRecent.Key);
    }

    Node added = new Node(key, value);
    cache[key] = added;
    AddToFront(added);
  }
}
```

A cache with capacity 1 always removes its current node before accepting a different key. Dictionary lookup and list changes are O(1) average, with O(capacity) space.

A short C++ version can use `std::list` instead of writing the linked list nodes by hand. The front of the list is the newest item, and the back is the item to remove next. The map stores an iterator for each key, so moving or deleting an item does not require a search.

```cpp
#include <list>
#include <unordered_map>
using namespace std;

class LRUCache {
  private:
    int capacity;
    list<pair<int, int>> items;
    unordered_map<int, list<pair<int, int>>::iterator> positions;

  public:
    LRUCache(int capacity) : capacity(capacity) {
    }

    int get(int key_) {
        auto found = positions.find(key_);
        if (found == positions.end()) {
            return -1;
        }

        items.splice(items.begin(), items, found->second);
        return found->second->second;
    }

    void put(int key_, int value) {
        auto found = positions.find(key_);

        if (found != positions.end()) {
            found->second->second = value;
            items.splice(items.begin(), items, found->second);
            return;
        }

        if (capacity == 0) {
            return;
        }

        if (static_cast<int>(items.size()) == capacity) {
            int oldKey = items.back().first;
            positions.erase(oldKey);
            items.pop_back();
        }

        items.emplace_front(key_, value);
        positions[key_] = items.begin();
    }
};
```

Both cache operations take average O(1) time. The cache stores at most `capacity` entries, so the space usage is O(capacity).

`OrderedDict` already supports moving an entry and removing the oldest entry in constant time. I keep the least recently used key at the beginning and the newest key at the end.

This avoids custom node code, but it depends on the standard library container rather than showing the list links directly.

```python
from collections import OrderedDict

class LRUCache:
    def __init__(self, capacity):
        self.capacity = capacity
        self.data = OrderedDict()

    def get(self, key_):
        if key_ not in self.data:
            return -1

        value = self.data[key_]
        self.data.move_to_end(key_)
        return value

    def put(self, key_, value):
        if self.capacity == 0:
            return

        if key_ in self.data:
            self.data[key_] = value
            self.data.move_to_end(key_)
            return

        if len(self.data) == self.capacity:
            self.data.popitem(last=False)

        self.data[key_] = value
```

The missing-key case returns `-1`, and putting an existing key updates it instead of creating a duplicate. `get` and `put` are O(1) average, while the stored entries use O(capacity) space.

The map gives the node for a key immediately. The linked list stores the usage order, with the front holding the most recently used item and the back holding the least recently used item. Moving a node only changes a few links.

Dry run with capacity 2:
`Put(1, 10)` gives `[1]`, then `Put(2, 20)` gives `[2, 1]`. Calling `Get(1)` changes it to `[1, 2]`, so `Put(3, 30)` removes key 2.

